  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 342 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nOn Wednesday, upper level ridging over the southeast US is expected \nto intensify, with ensemble mean 500 mb height progs showing values \naround 600 dm over the TN Valley Wednesday afternoon. The subsidence \nassociated with the growing heat dome should cause the sfc cold \nfront to the north of the Ohio Valley to dissipate and lift back to \nthe north, suppressing precipitation chances and reinforcing the \nunseasonably hot conditions. There is high confidence in unusually \nhot temperatures continuing into Thursday, as ECMWF Extreme Forecast \nIndex values for high temperatures exceed 0.95, with a Shift of \nTails around 1. Highs are expected to range from 93-97 across the \narea Wednesday and Thursday, with peak afternoon heat indices in the \nupper 90s and low 100s. Limited nighttime relief will exacerbate the \neffects of this late season heat surge, with lows only expected in \nthe low-to-mid 70s through the week. As confidence in sustained hot \ntemperatures continues later into the week, the heat advisory may \nneed to be extended in time.\n\nAs we head into Friday and the weekend, the upper height anomalies \nover the southeast US will gradually break down from a combination \nof shortwave troughs riding across the northern US and a tropical \nwave slowly moving across the Gulf. However, recent runs of model \nguidance have trended toward persistence of the -PNA pattern with \nsplit flow across the western CONUS, which will limit the ability \nfor troughing to dig across the eastern US. While the breakdown of \nthe ridge should allow for a low (10-30%) chance of afternoon \nshowers and storms to return, a substantial cooldown appears less \nlikely through the weekend. With that being said, there is a notable \nuptick in spread in high temperatures over the weekend, possibly due \nto increased potential for clouds and precipitation to limit \nwarming. A drier solution over the weekend would support continued \nhighs in the mid-90s, potentially extending this heat wave.",
